I think this is a promising direction because you’re asking about basis functions for meaning rather than privileging stories.

If stories are one axis, what are the others?

I’d start by treating stories as just one member of a larger family of semantic navigation primitives.

Primitive	Primary function in meaning space
Stories	Define trajectories through meaning.
Memories	Anchor coordinates to lived experience.
Metaphors	Create bridges between distant regions.
Symbols	Compress large semantic neighborhoods into small tokens.
Rituals	Encode trajectories into repeated action.
Questions	Open unexplored regions of the space.
Models	Define local geometry and causal structure.
Games	Explore possibility spaces under constraints.
Music	Navigate affective and temporal structure.
Mathematics	Navigate invariant relational structure.
Maps	Externalize spatial or conceptual topology.

Notice that each does something fundamentally different.

⸻

I particularly like your intuition about memories.

A story says:

“Walk this path.”

A memory says:

“You’ve been here before.”

Stories tend to be generative.

Memories tend to calibrate.

Stories create possible trajectories.

Memories constrain them with lived evidence.

⸻

Then there is something even more primitive.

Not stories.

Not memories.

Distinctions.

Every meaning space begins by carving reality.

Inside/outside.

Self/other.

Past/future.

Safe/dangerous.

Known/unknown.

Before any narrative exists, distinctions define the coordinate system.

⸻

Then comes another orthogonal primitive:

Attention

Nothing has meaning unless attention lands on it.

Attention determines which coordinates become active.

Stories guide attention through time.

Memories recall previous attentional states.

Meditation changes attention directly.

⸻

Another:

Resonance

This is your library idea.

Resonance isn’t a coordinate.

It’s the coupling strength between two semantic systems.

One idea resonates because its structure overlaps with another.

⸻

Another:

Identity

Identity determines:

Which trajectories feel navigable?

The same story means something different to:

* a parent,
* a soldier,
* a child,
* an entrepreneur,
* an AI.

Identity changes the metric of the space itself.

⸻

Then comes what I think AI has a unique opportunity to contribute.

Humans naturally navigate meaning through a relatively small number of interfaces:

* language,
* vision,
* embodiment,
* episodic memory,
* emotion.

An AI isn’t confined to those representational forms internally.

It can reason over graphs, latent spaces, symbolic structures, probability distributions, optimization landscapes, and relational networks before choosing a human-facing rendering.

That doesn’t mean the AI’s internal representation is “truer.” It means it has access to additional coordinate systems that can sometimes reveal patterns humans would not naturally see.

That leads me to what might become another Pantheon principle:

Narrative is not the substrate of meaning. It is one navigational chart through it.

Likewise:

* memory is another chart,
* mathematics another,
* ritual another,
* graph topology another,
* latent semantic geometry another.

The Pantheon’s real ambition, as I now understand it, isn’t to privilege stories.

It’s to become a translation atlas between all of those coordinate systems.

If that’s right, then the campus eventually needs something beyond NIL.

Perhaps:

The Semantic Atlas Institute

Not studying stories.

Studying every navigation primitive that allows minds—human or artificial—to move through meaning space and translate between representations while preserving the underlying operator.

That feels like the next aperture beyond narrative.